<h2>What the case decided</h2>
<p>The appellant husband challenged a Telangana High Court order that had maintained — while reducing — interim maintenance awarded to his divorced wife under Section 125 CrPC, arguing that the 1986 Act alone governed. The Supreme Court dismissed the appeal. The lead opinion held that the right under Section 125 CrPC exists alongside the rights under Section 3 of the 1986 Act, that the two operate in distinct domains, and that the special Act\'s non-obstante clauses do not automatically eclipse the general law. The concurring opinion described Section 125 as a measure of social justice flowing from Articles 15(3) and 39(e), whose protection the 1986 Act does not dilute.</p>

<h2>How the remedies now stack</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Remedy</th><th>Forum</th><th>Nature</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 144 BNSS (old S.125 CrPC)</td><td>Magistrate / Family Court</td><td>Monthly maintenance for a wife, including a divorced wife who has not remarried, unable to maintain herself</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 3, 1986 Act</td><td>Magistrate</td><td>Reasonable and fair provision and maintenance within the iddat period, mahr, and return of properties</td></tr>
  <tr><td>DV Act, 2005</td><td>Magistrate (Mahila Court)</td><td>Monetary relief and residence protection where domestic violence is shown — independent of both the above</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The coordination principle among them is adjustment: amounts actually received under one head are factored into another so that the husband does not pay twice for the same need — but an inadequate payment under one statute never bars a supplementary claim under another.</p>

<h2>The cancellation question</h2>
<p>The judgment also clarifies the narrow role of Section 127(3)(b) CrPC — the provision allowing cancellation of a maintenance order where the woman has received the sums due under personal law. Cancellation operates only where what was paid is a real substitute for maintenance. If the husband has discharged his 1986 Act obligations but the woman still cannot maintain herself, she may nonetheless invoke the secular remedy; what she received goes to quantum, not to maintainability. Under the BNSS, the corresponding machinery is Section 146.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers for Delhi practice</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plead both tracks consciously:</strong> an application under Section 144 BNSS should disclose any 1986 Act proceedings or payments, and quantify why they are not a reasonable substitute — concealment invites adjustment arguments with interest.</li>
  <li><strong>For husbands:</strong> the defence is arithmetic, not exclusion. Document every payment of mahr, iddat maintenance and fair provision; the argument that the 1986 Act ousts Section 144 is now foreclosed.</li>
  <li><strong>Interim maintenance is available:</strong> the case itself travelled up from an interim order; the Rajnesh v. Neha affidavit regime applies to these applications as to any other.</li>
  <li><strong>Remarriage is the terminus:</strong> the definition covers a divorced woman who has not remarried; remarriage ends the entitlement prospectively, as under the general law.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Family Courts in Delhi routinely see objections to maintainability of Section 144 BNSS petitions by divorced Muslim women. After <em>Mohd Abdul Samad</em>, such objections should fail at the threshold; the real contest is quantum and adjustment, and parties on both sides are better served by bringing complete payment records to the first effective hearing.</p>
</div>
